Results of lab 2:

Graph 1: Marital Status by Age, Race, and Region
Across all four regions, the share of respondents who are married or previously married increases substantially with age, with the largest jump occurring between ages 17–38 and 39–52. Black respondents consistently have the lowest percentages across age groups and regions, while differences among racial groups narrow considerably at older ages.

Graph 2: Marriage Status by Race and Region
Regional differences are relatively modest compared with racial differences, with the South generally showing higher married/previously married percentages and the Northeast generally lower percentages. Across every region, White respondents have the highest percentages, Black respondents the lowest, and Asian and other-race respondents fall between the two.

Graph 3: Married/Previously Married vs. Never Married
I got curious to see if there was a difference between 'married' and 'never married' by race and region. Never married status is most common among younger respondents and declines sharply with age across all racial groups. Black respondents have the highest never married percentage in every age group, while White and Asian respondents generally have the highest married/previously married percentages.

Overall: I originally thought region would explain differences in marital status by race, but that didn’t really pan out because the regional patterns were pretty similar. So I wanted to see if age played an additional factor. Never married rates are much higher among younger respondents and drop pretty sharply with age, while married/previously married rates increase, which was as expected. But even within the same age groups, there are still noticeable differences by race, especially for Black respondents, so one can aruge that age may have a pattern but not definitive enough to state a relationship.

